  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nPeriods of rain will continue to impact the area this \nafternoon. Rainfall amounts of 1-2 inches have been observed \nacross NE PA, and the precipitation shield continues to drift \nnorthward across the area.\n\nWith the center of the low sliding just to the south later today,\ncolder air will begin to fill in from north allowing rain to \nbecome a rain/snow mix and eventually just snow overnight. \nTiming of the precipitation type transition will depend on how \nquickly the colder air moves in. Rain will change over to snow \nin the higher elevations first but most valleys should see snow \nby Friday morning. With surface temperatures stuck in the 30s, \nsnow continues through much of the daytime Friday thanks to \nwraparound moisture on the back side of the low. As the low \ndrifts out to sea later on Friday, coverage will become more \nscattered and showery in nature on the back side of the system. \nMeanwhile, low-level flow will advect colder air over the Great \nLakes, generating lake effect snow.\n\nSnowfall totals have been creeping upwards since the previous \nforecast. Higher elevations of the Catskills still could see the\nhighest totals with localized amounts of 3+ inches. Other \nhigher elevations across the region could see an inch or more. A\ncoating of snow is possible elsewhere on colder surfaces but is\nnot expected to accumulate much on the warmer ground. If rain \ntransitions to snow quicker than expected, then final snowfall \namounts could end up higher. These forecasted totals are\napproaching advisory level, but confidence remains too low for \nany headline issuance at this time.\n\nTemperatures through the end of the work week will be colder than \npast days. Highs today will be in the 40s and 50s before falling to \nnear and below freezing tonight. Temperatures will struggle to warm \nup Friday, only maxing out in the 30s to low 40s. Then sub-freezing \ntemperatures are expected Friday night and this could lead to light \nicing on untreated, wet surfaces.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFlow will become northwesterly and 850mb temperatures fall colder on \nSaturday, leading to lake effect precipitation lingering into at \nleast Sunday. Along the northern areas of the Finger Lakes, \nprecipitation will be primarily rain showers or a rain/snow mix\ndue to slightly warmer conditions. Elsewhere, precipitation \nwill be mainly snow showers, at least on Saturday.\n\nEventually, drier air begins to move in early next week as an \nupper level ridge approaches the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAfter this brief sample of winter, weather conditions improve during \nthe midweek period. The aforementioned ridge builds into the region \nTuesday with high pressure present through at least Thursday. \nTemperatures will trend warmer and back above average, at least\nfor daytime highs. The nights will remain cool with \ntemperatures near or below freezing.",
